The current implementation can be found under org.kbanerjee.concurrent.DistributedLock. Its fairly simple and for the case where several
local locks try to acquire it will save you the network overhead while maintaining fairness locally and somewhat arguably even remotely.

Its drawback are as follows:
<A> NO LOCK PARKING
    Consider the only acquires come from one machine but they are sparse such that the sequence is lock-unlock lock-unlock,
    this would be the worst case where each lock/unlock call will talk to the network server

<B> ARBITRARY QUANTUM
    The larger problem is that the check if global lock is contended is a source of network overhead especially if there is no
    contention. There really is no clean solution under the current model, you could increase the quantum value but then it can
    negatively impact network fairness. 


But really <A> is the larger problem. You could solve it with another timer based quantum but that assumes back to back lock
calls come within a window. The counter argument being if they dont come wihtin a window making the extra network call should be
fine, its really that you dont want to jam the network not avoid it at all costs.

