 ALTERNATE SOLUTION
==============================================================================
<> The above solution works on the "ask_server_if_it_needs_back_the_lock" principle. 
   In the case of unlock we conservatively give the lock back with the assumption
   that the Server (read other peers) *MAY* need the lock.


<> If we reversed the princple where you acquire the lock and keep it with yourself.
   If another peer wants it let him get my address from the server, and queue a RPC
   in my local queue. When the RPC executes it reverts my lock ownership and returns
   whereafter the remote machine is the current lock owner.


<> This should remove <A> and <B> entirely while we implement a separate lock.revoke() function that will be invoked
   by remote clients using RPC (or RMI or SOAP or place your favorite middleware OO technology here)


<> Q: So what does globalLock.lock() do then?
   A: It atomically replaces the current owner with the requesting owner. outside this atomicity it thereafter 
      queues a RPC on the last owner's machine and waits for it to complete.


<> Q: Hmm so it changes ownership before the new node actually gets lock ownership? Weird so what happens if before the RPC
      completes another node requests for the globalLock?
   A: It will find the new node as the lock owner and queue a RPC to succeed on this new node. Effectively the queue has been
      moved from a single queue of contending machines on the server to distributed queues on a peer to peer basis.


<> Q: But there is one drawback yet. Global lock acquisitions in the contended case will involve two network hops as against
      one.
   A: Yes that is correct. But read below for an optimization.


<> If a node is requesting a global lock for the first time and the global lock is held by some other machine nothing can 
   prevent two network hops.

   But if its requesting for the lock because its lock was stolen by another machine then that can be solved by the stealing
   RPC detecting multilpe local contenders and taking a localLock and queueing the unlock as a RPC call in its own queue.
   So to the local machine it looks like a local lock that takes way toooo long to complete.


<> Q: Isnt there a global fairness problem? Consider what you said before, once you claim ownership even before you get it
      another node could request ownership by queueing on your machine. But you are placing the stolen_from_machine in your
      own queue so ...?
   A: Nopes the additions to the local queue are serialized and in FIFO order so if we are waiting for long before being able
      to successfully steal the lock, the additions will respect temporal order.

